1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Have you ever thought, if you could predict the future,

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,919
use very precise logic to predict your competitors,

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:00:11,279
or your colleagues, even politicians' next moves,

4
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:12,280
what would that be like.

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:15,679
Wow, that sounds a bit like mind-reading in sci-fi movies.

6
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:17,879
Right, that's what I thought at first too.

7
00:00:17,879 --> 00:00:20,600
Imagine, you no longer rely on intuition to guess,

8
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:23,000
nor do you need to read micro-expressions,

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,080
but rather, how to say, have a tool that can directly see through the cards of human decision-making.

10
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,120
Actually, this is definitely not some mysticism.

11
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:34,120
Behind it is a very rigorous logic system,

12
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:38,760
which is the core we'll discuss in today's deep dive — game theory.

13
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:43,000
We recently translated those classic game theory course materials from Yale University,

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,280
and their after-class exercises, and found a huge treasure trove hidden inside.

15
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:48,960
Right, a huge treasure trove,

16
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,920
and our task today is to, for beginners like you,

17
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:55,799
peel away the scary-looking hard math course that is game theory.

18
00:00:55,799 --> 00:00:56,560
Yes,

19
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,320
much of the human behavior we find incomprehensible.

20
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,560
You see, like endless price wars, or climate summits that achieve nothing,

21
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,320
through the lens of game theory, all reveal a cold,

22
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:09,359
but extremely precise inevitability.

23
00:01:09,359 --> 00:01:13,000
It really is inevitability. Don't worry, this isn't that kind of boring theory course

24
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,240
that requires blackboard calculus.

25
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,599
Once you peel off the shell, game theory is essentially the ultimate art of thinking from others' perspective.

26
00:01:20,599 --> 00:01:21,480
Right, putting yourself in others' shoes.

27
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,800
But, before bringing this theory into your daily life,

28
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:27,280
we first need to figure out what a game actually is.

29
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,319
Taking a casual stroll on the street — that's not a game.

30
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:33,120
The course materials especially emphasized three essential indicators.

31
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,039
Three indicators, all indispensable,

32
00:01:35,039 --> 00:01:37,599
namely players, strategies, and payoffs.

33
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:38,239
Tell us quickly.

34
00:01:38,239 --> 00:01:42,759
No problem. Players are the ones making decisions. Strategies are the cards in your hand,

35
00:01:42,759 --> 00:01:45,439
for example, you can choose to cooperate, or betray.

36
00:01:45,439 --> 00:01:47,879
And the most core one is actually the third — payoffs.

37
00:01:47,879 --> 00:01:48,560
Right.

38
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:52,879
If we just list you a bunch of strategy options without telling you what making these choices

39
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:54,599
would bring in benefits or harms.

40
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,840
Honestly, at most it's just a menu.

41
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:58,360
Haha, ordering from a menu, right.

42
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:01,400
Only when your choices can actually affect how much money you ultimately get,

43
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,680
or how many votes you obtain, or even how many hours of sleep you get.

44
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:07,280
then payoffs become the underlying logic driving all behavior.

45
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,920
So, if we point the magnifying glass at these two words, payoffs,

46
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:14,120
many headache-inducing real-world problems suddenly make sense.

47
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,920
Take the most classic prisoner's dilemma, for example.

48
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:19,520
Everyone knows that if both sides cooperate, both win.

49
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:24,080
But in reality, people always unhesitatingly walk hand in hand toward double loss.

50
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:29,360
It really is like that. For example, companies are now frantically fighting price wars.

51
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,800
Two companies slash prices desperately to grab market share,

52
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,000
and in the end, the entire industry's profits are wiped out.

53
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,240
As a consumer, you might feel great,

54
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,800
but for companies, this is a complete disaster.

55
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:43,960
Absolutely a disaster. And the carbon emission problem is the same.

56
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:48,040
Right, every country knows that continuing to pollute will destroy the earth,

57
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:50,320
but why can't they stop?

58
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:53,840
Because from the perspective of each individual country,

59
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:56,200
its payoff structure determines that

60
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,319
the free-rider strategy is the most rational choice.

61
00:02:59,319 --> 00:03:00,520
How does one free-ride?

62
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:05,360
Think about it. If other countries all put great effort into reducing emissions and the climate improves,

63
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,680
then I can enjoy good weather without doing anything,

64
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,879
while not affecting my industrial development.

65
00:03:09,879 --> 00:03:11,159
Simply winning big.

66
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:14,920
Right. Conversely, if other countries don't reduce emissions,

67
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:18,240
then even if I grit my teeth and reduce, not only can I not save the earth,

68
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,080
I'll also cause my own domestic economy to collapse.

69
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,120
So, no matter what others do,

70
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:25,599
continuing to emit is the highest payoff for me.

71
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:30,480
Wow, hearing this, I instantly remember the nightmare I hated most in college,

72
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:32,080
group projects.

73
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:36,000
Great, this example is so classic, it perfectly recreates the prisoner's dilemma.

74
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,280
Right? At first everyone sat around in the library,

75
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:43,400
swearing solemnly, brothers, this time we absolutely won't slack off,

76
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,960
everyone goes home and does proper research to get an A.

77
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:46,680
And then?

78
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:49,319
And then the night before the final submission,

79
00:03:49,319 --> 00:03:51,000
you open the shared document,

80
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,799
and it's all a pile of copy-pasted garbage.

81
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:57,840
I just wonder, since everyone sat together and said everything openly,

82
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,599
and we all know that getting an A benefits everyone,

83
00:04:00,599 --> 00:04:03,079
why does this heartfelt communication

84
00:04:03,079 --> 00:04:05,280
have absolutely no effect?

85
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:09,400
Because language communication itself didn't change your payoffs at all.

86
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:10,879
Hey, that hits hard.

87
00:04:10,879 --> 00:04:13,639
No matter how many solemn vows you made in the library,

88
00:04:13,639 --> 00:04:17,000
as long as the pleasure of lying on the sofa playing games at home

89
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,800
is still greater than the sense of achievement from painfully reading papers,

90
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,800
then those promises are just a piece of scrap paper.

91
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,040
Indeed, scrap paper.

92
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:24,680
It's the same at climate summits,

93
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:27,000
politicians can talk until the cows come home,

94
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,279
but as long as the individual payoff of driving a gas-guzzling car at home is still higher than environmental protection,

95
00:04:31,279 --> 00:04:32,959
high emissions will definitely continue.

96
00:04:32,959 --> 00:04:35,600
In other words, as long as the incentive structure doesn't change,

97
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,079
pure talk has no binding power at all.

98
00:04:38,079 --> 00:04:40,480
To break this lose-lose situation,

99
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,000
we can't rely on moral appeals,

100
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,560
but rather on contracts, international treaties, or rules that deduct credit points.

101
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:50,680
Right, actually raising the cost of slacking off to change the entire payoff matrix.

102
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,439
This logic is very clear.

103
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:56,639
So, since payoffs and incentive structures determine how opponents will act,

104
00:04:56,639 --> 00:05:00,759
we can actually reverse-engineer this to predict their next moves, right?

105
00:05:00,759 --> 00:05:02,000
That's exactly the logic.

106
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,240
This brings us to the first law of game theory:

107
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:08,639
rational people never choose dominated strategies.

108
00:05:08,639 --> 00:05:09,879
Dominated strategies?

109
00:05:09,879 --> 00:05:11,480
Right, to make it more intuitive,

110
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:14,759
we can use a historical case from the Yale course materials to reason it out.

111
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:17,480
Let's say it's the third century BC,

112
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:21,000
the Carthaginian general Hannibal is preparing to invade Rome.

113
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,759
Oh, I know this. Hannibal has two routes to choose,

114
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:28,920
one is the flat, easy coastal route,

115
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:32,560
the other is the extremely dangerous Alps mountain range.

116
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:35,759
Yes. And for the defending side, Rome,

117
00:05:35,759 --> 00:05:39,240
with limited troops, can only heavily defend one of the two.

118
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:44,000
So if we stand in the Roman general's shoes and think with game theory,

119
00:05:44,279 --> 00:05:46,360
if Hannibal takes the coast route,

120
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:48,199
as long as Rome doesn't defend it,

121
00:05:48,199 --> 00:05:51,319
he can march his army through completely unharmed.

122
00:05:51,319 --> 00:05:53,480
But if he takes the Alps?

123
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,279
Whether Rome defends it or not,

124
00:05:55,279 --> 00:05:57,680
he would have to, due to severe cold and avalanches,

125
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:00,040
first lose a third of his troops.

126
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:02,160
We call this putting yourself in the other's shoes.

127
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:05,800
The Romans, standing in Hannibal's position, calculated the accounts.

128
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:10,600
Taking the coast, the worst outcome is a head-on clash with the Roman legions,

129
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:12,879
the best outcome is passing through smoothly.

130
00:06:12,879 --> 00:06:17,839
Taking the Alps, no matter what, would first suffer serious non-combat attrition.

131
00:06:17,839 --> 00:06:20,439
Right, so in the game theory data,

132
00:06:20,439 --> 00:06:25,199
taking the Alps is a weakly dominated strategy for Hannibal.

133
00:06:25,199 --> 00:06:27,240
Because in the vast majority of cases,

134
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,959
his payoff would be worse than taking the coast,

135
00:06:29,959 --> 00:06:32,759
or at best equal, but absolutely never better.

136
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:37,439
So, based on the assumption that rational people never choose dominated strategies,

137
00:06:37,439 --> 00:06:39,519
Rome reached the only conclusion:

138
00:06:39,519 --> 00:06:42,279
Hannibal would definitely take the coast route.

139
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:44,720
So they deployed their heavy troops there.

140
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:48,840
The logic is perfectly sound, but the script of history wasn't written that way at all.

141
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:52,160
Right, Hannibal just refused to play by the rules.

142
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,800
He really led his army across the deadly Alps,

143
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,160
and caught Rome completely off guard.

144
00:06:58,160 --> 00:06:59,360
What does this show?

145
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:01,080
It shows that in reality, opponents,

146
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:05,480
sometimes deliberately use your rational calculation to set a reverse trap.

147
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:09,600
This is precisely the most fascinating part of game theory in real-world application.

148
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,200
To explore this gap between theory and reality,

149
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:14,800
the Yale classroom often plays a game,

150
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:17,080
called 'guess two-thirds of the average'.

151
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:18,760
How do you play this game?

152
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:19,840
The rules are simple.

153
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:24,600
Now the whole class picks any integer between one and one hundred and writes it down.

154
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,439
Whoever picks the number closest to

155
00:07:26,439 --> 00:07:29,600
two-thirds of the average of all the class's numbers,

156
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:31,080
wins the five dollars.

157
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:33,360
Wait, let's break down this logic.

158
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:37,400
Let's use the same method of eliminating dominated strategies we used before to reason it out.

159
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:38,480
Okay, you try.

160
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:41,800
First, suppose the whole class has gone crazy,

161
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,360
everyone picks the largest number, one hundred.

162
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:46,680
Then the class average is one hundred,

163
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:48,920
two-thirds of it is sixty-six point six,

164
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:50,160
which rounds to about sixty-seven.

165
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:50,759
Right.

166
00:07:50,759 --> 00:07:53,400
Numbers above that, like eighty or ninety,

167
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,319
are meaningless in this game.

168
00:07:55,319 --> 00:07:57,040
They directly become dominated strategies.

169
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:58,520
So as a rational player,

170
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:00,439
you would erase all numbers greater than sixty-seven

171
00:08:00,439 --> 00:08:02,160
from your mind.

172
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:03,759
But that's the first level.

173
00:08:03,759 --> 00:08:04,800
There's a second level.

174
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:07,400
Since numbers above sixty-seven are dominated strategies,

175
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:10,440
then every rational student won't choose them.

176
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:11,320
What does this mean?

177
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:12,920
It means wait...

178
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:17,680
it means the maximum number the class can choose is only sixty-seven.

179
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:20,840
So the new average can at most be sixty-seven.

180
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:24,120
And two-thirds of sixty-seven is about forty-five.

181
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:27,240
Right, so numbers between forty-six and sixty-seven

182
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,960
have now also become impossible-to-win dominated strategies,

183
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:30,720
all eliminated.

184
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:35,280
Wow, this elimination is like Russian dolls nesting inside each other.

185
00:08:35,279 --> 00:08:38,439
Since everyone won't choose numbers above forty-five,

186
00:08:38,439 --> 00:08:40,799
then the new target number becomes thirty.

187
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:43,519
Since no one will choose thirty, the target becomes twenty.

188
00:08:43,519 --> 00:08:46,240
If you keep reasoning infinitely with this logic,

189
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:47,519
layer by layer, like peeling an onion,

190
00:08:47,519 --> 00:08:51,600
finally the whole class is left with only one logically perfect number.

191
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,799
Yes, that number is one.

192
00:08:53,799 --> 00:08:56,279
Only when everyone picks one,

193
00:08:56,279 --> 00:08:58,159
two-thirds of one is approximately one,

194
00:08:58,159 --> 00:09:00,399
can this infinite loop stop.

195
00:09:00,399 --> 00:09:02,360
But there's a huge bug here.

196
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:03,360
What bug?

197
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:06,080
If we play this game in real life,

198
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:08,320
I absolutely would not pick one.

199
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:10,680
Because if there's just one person in the class who didn't wake up,

200
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:13,360
or someone deliberately messing around who casually picks one hundred,

201
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,320
the average gets instantly pulled up.

202
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,159
The person who picked one would lose badly.

203
00:09:17,159 --> 00:09:21,399
Right, people aren't emotionless random number generators.

204
00:09:21,399 --> 00:09:22,800
Your intuition is very sharp.

205
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:26,440
Actually, the real result when they played this game in the Yale classroom back then was,

206
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,639
the class average was about thirteen,

207
00:09:28,639 --> 00:09:30,879
and the eventual winner picked the number nine.

208
00:09:30,879 --> 00:09:33,159
It never fell to the theoretical middle point of one.

209
00:09:33,159 --> 00:09:34,159
Not at all.

210
00:09:34,159 --> 00:09:36,399
There's a decisive factor hidden here,

211
00:09:36,399 --> 00:09:39,039
which in game theory is called common knowledge.

212
00:09:39,039 --> 00:09:42,600
Common knowledge, it sounds like saying common sense that everyone knows.

213
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:43,919
It's not just common sense.

214
00:09:43,919 --> 00:09:46,439
In the game we just played, to dare to pick one,

215
00:09:46,439 --> 00:09:49,199
the premise is not only that you yourself must be perfectly rational,

216
00:09:49,199 --> 00:09:51,719
you also have to believe that all your classmates are rational.

217
00:09:51,719 --> 00:09:52,719
That's already hard enough.

218
00:09:52,719 --> 00:09:53,639
Not only that,

219
00:09:53,639 --> 00:09:57,639
you also have to believe that all your classmates believe that you believe they are rational,

220
00:09:57,639 --> 00:09:58,639
and so on,

221
00:09:58,639 --> 00:10:01,120
this chain of trust must extend infinitely.

222
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:03,720
Oh my god, this sounds so convoluted.

223
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:07,080
To explain how hard it is to achieve this common knowledge in reality,

224
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:10,560
scholars proposed a thought experiment with pink hats.

225
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:11,480
Let's talk about it.

226
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:13,840
Suppose two people stand on stage,

227
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:16,360
put a hat on each of them,

228
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:17,519
and tell them,

229
00:10:17,519 --> 00:10:20,960
at least one of you is wearing a pink hat.

230
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:23,039
Okay, at least one pink hat.

231
00:10:23,039 --> 00:10:26,360
Then they open their eyes and look at each other.

232
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:30,600
Both can clearly see that the other's hat is pink.

233
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:34,399
At this point, that the other person is wearing a pink hat — for each of them,

234
00:10:34,399 --> 00:10:35,560
is it known?

235
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:36,759
Of course it's known.

236
00:10:36,759 --> 00:10:38,279
I see your hat,

237
00:10:38,279 --> 00:10:40,840
but I don't know whether you saw my hat.

238
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:42,759
If my hat is white,

239
00:10:42,759 --> 00:10:44,879
that means you could only see a white hat.

240
00:10:44,879 --> 00:10:47,080
Right, I know the facts,

241
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:49,159
and I know that you know the facts,

242
00:10:49,159 --> 00:10:50,680
but I can't be certain

243
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:52,560
whether you know that I know.

244
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:55,440
Once this nested knowledge structure breaks,

245
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,680
the logical chain of iteratively eliminating strategies also breaks.

246
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:00,000
So in reality,

247
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,440
when you can't assume everyone will, like computers,

248
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:05,160
reason infinitely,

249
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:06,560
this tool fails.

250
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:08,960
Yes, we have to put it away,

251
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:12,040
and switch to a more powerful, more practical concept:

252
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:13,440
best response.

253
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,800
Best response.

254
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,520
This term sounds very straightforward.

255
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:19,520
Does it mean, based on others' decisions,

256
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,600
I find a response that's most beneficial to me.

257
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:23,040
That's exactly what it means.

258
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:25,000
The core of best response is that

259
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,760
you no longer need to guess whether

260
00:11:26,759 --> 00:11:28,679
others are infinitely rational.

261
00:11:28,679 --> 00:11:30,000
Then what do I need to do?

262
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,559
You just need to form an expectation about

263
00:11:32,559 --> 00:11:34,120
what others might do,

264
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:35,679
and then, based on that expectation,

265
00:11:35,679 --> 00:11:38,679
choose the action that brings you the maximum payoff.

266
00:11:38,679 --> 00:11:42,639
We can use a very famous theorem in political science, the median voter theorem,

267
00:11:42,639 --> 00:11:44,080
to see how it works.

268
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:45,960
Political science, that's quite a crossover!

269
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:48,080
Actually, the underlying logic is the same.

270
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:50,879
Suppose there's a political spectrum from one to ten,

271
00:11:50,879 --> 00:11:52,279
one represents the far left,

272
00:11:52,279 --> 00:11:53,879
ten represents the far right,

273
00:11:53,879 --> 00:11:56,759
voters are evenly distributed across these ten notches.

274
00:11:56,759 --> 00:11:59,200
Now two politicians are running in the election.

275
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:00,039
The rule is,

276
00:12:00,039 --> 00:12:02,200
voters will definitely vote for

277
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:04,559
the candidate whose position is closest to their own.

278
00:12:04,559 --> 00:12:06,279
So if I pick one,

279
00:12:06,279 --> 00:12:08,360
the most extreme left wing,

280
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:10,080
and my opponent picks two,

281
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:14,439
then my opponent will take all the voters from notch three to ten,

282
00:12:14,439 --> 00:12:16,039
because they're all closer to him.

283
00:12:16,039 --> 00:12:19,279
Right, you only get those pitiful votes at notch one.

284
00:12:19,279 --> 00:12:20,879
So I immediately understand that

285
00:12:20,879 --> 00:12:23,840
the extremes, one and ten, absolutely cannot be chosen.

286
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:25,920
Since the extreme positions can't be chosen,

287
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:30,240
the two politicians will start making best responses to each other's positions.

288
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:32,560
You pick two, I pick three to squeeze you.

289
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:33,800
You see I picked three,

290
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,720
you'll pick four to surround me.

291
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:37,960
In this game of fighting for votes,

292
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:40,720
their positions will ultimately, like magnets,

293
00:12:40,720 --> 00:12:42,800
keep converging toward the center,

294
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,399
and finally both squeeze together at positions five and six,

295
00:12:45,399 --> 00:12:47,600
desperately fighting for this group of swing voters.

296
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:50,360
Wow, this explains so much in history.

297
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:51,840
Many campaign strategies,

298
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:57,040
like Kennedy and Nixon, or Clinton, at the critical stage of elections,

299
00:12:57,040 --> 00:13:01,120
they often blur out their camp's most radical slogans.

300
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:05,200
Yes, and instead use moderate policies to court the middle.

301
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:08,920
We're just objectively using these cases to explain the model,

302
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:10,440
with no political bias.

303
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:13,120
Of course, we're just discussing game theory.

304
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:15,399
This logic is truly pervasive.

305
00:13:15,399 --> 00:13:16,920
Not only does it explain politics,

306
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:20,000
it also explains why on the same commercial street,

307
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,759
two competing gas stations or cafes

308
00:13:22,759 --> 00:13:25,720
never open evenly at the two ends of the street.

309
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:30,000
But insist on being huddled right together, opening at the very center of the street.

310
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,960
Everyone is squeezing each other's living space toward the middle.

311
00:13:32,960 --> 00:13:34,399
It's truly amazing.

312
00:13:34,399 --> 00:13:36,080
But speaking of best response,

313
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:39,240
that data about penalty shootouts in the materials,

314
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:41,679
is what really makes you gasp.

315
00:13:41,679 --> 00:13:48,559
Penalty shootouts are considered by many economists to be the most perfect laboratory for studying game theory in the real world.

316
00:13:48,559 --> 00:13:49,720
Why do you say that?

317
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:54,279
For the striker, there are three shooting directions: left, center, right.

318
00:13:54,279 --> 00:13:55,720
For the goalkeeper,

319
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:58,160
the diving directions are generally left and right, two of them.

320
00:13:58,160 --> 00:14:02,600
The course showed a set of real statistics for high-level professional players.

321
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:03,879
What does the data say?

322
00:14:03,879 --> 00:14:05,800
If the striker shoots to the left,

323
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:08,240
and the goalkeeper also happens to dive left,

324
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:09,920
because the goalkeeper guessed right,

325
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:12,560
the scoring rate is only about 40%.

326
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:14,600
But if the goalkeeper dives the wrong way,

327
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:15,560
to the right,

328
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:18,360
the scoring rate soars to over 90%.

329
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:20,720
The right side data is basically similar, right?

330
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:21,879
Now here's the key point.

331
00:14:21,879 --> 00:14:23,800
If in this life-or-death moment,

332
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:26,000
the striker goes against the grain,

333
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,320
and directly kicks the ball to the very center of the goal?

334
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:29,919
The data is very cold.

335
00:14:29,919 --> 00:14:32,480
Given this set of professional players' data,

336
00:14:32,480 --> 00:14:35,519
whether the goalkeeper dives left or right,

337
00:14:35,519 --> 00:14:37,759
as long as the striker kicks the ball down the middle,

338
00:14:37,759 --> 00:14:40,279
the scoring rate is always stable at around 60%.

339
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:41,159
Guessing 60%?

340
00:14:41,159 --> 00:14:42,039
Yes.

341
00:14:42,039 --> 00:14:44,080
If you draw a chart in your mind,

342
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:46,600
this line representing the win rate of shooting center,

343
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:49,639
is always pressed below the left and right lines.

344
00:14:49,639 --> 00:14:50,680
This means,

345
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:53,040
no matter how you and the goalkeeper act,

346
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:56,360
shooting center can never bring the highest expected payoff.

347
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:59,200
So the conclusion is that in professional-level penalty shootouts,

348
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,879
kicking the ball to the center is absolutely not a best response.

349
00:15:01,879 --> 00:15:04,399
Right, absolutely don't kick the ball to the center.

350
00:15:04,399 --> 00:15:06,040
But wait,

351
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:07,920
what if I don't play by the rules?

352
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:08,879
What do you mean?

353
00:15:08,879 --> 00:15:12,040
What if I'm not one of those professional stars who shoot precisely wherever they aim,

354
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:15,000
I'm just an amateur with particularly bad skills,

355
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,399
who only knows 'power creates miracles'.

356
00:15:16,399 --> 00:15:17,919
Good, power creates miracles.

357
00:15:17,919 --> 00:15:22,360
Right, if I insist on aiming for the left or right corner of the goal,

358
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:25,279
there's a high probability that my own footwork is bad,

359
00:15:25,279 --> 00:15:27,799
and I'd directly kick the ball into the stands,

360
00:15:27,799 --> 00:15:30,319
with a scoring rate that might not even reach 20%.

361
00:15:30,319 --> 00:15:35,159
Instead, if I close my eyes and blast the ball with all my strength toward the very center of the goal,

362
00:15:35,159 --> 00:15:38,159
at least I can guarantee the ball stays within the goal frame.

363
00:15:38,159 --> 00:15:38,959
At this point,

364
00:15:38,959 --> 00:15:41,600
isn't kicking the center the best choice?

365
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,919
The point you raise is simply brilliant.

366
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:48,480
This is precisely the most critical step in economic modeling.

367
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:53,120
The thinking you just showed vividly demonstrates what it means to enrich the model.

368
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:54,280
Enrich the model?

369
00:15:54,280 --> 00:15:57,760
Right, our previous conclusion was built on the variable that

370
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:59,800
kicking to the left or right won't result in mistakes.

371
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:02,160
Once we add the new variable you mentioned,

372
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:04,840
that is, shooting with great power

373
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:07,800
but extremely low precision, into the model,

374
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:09,840
the base scoring rate of kicking to the sides

375
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:12,960
will drop off a cliff because of the risk of kicking wide.

376
00:16:12,960 --> 00:16:15,280
With that, the chart is different.

377
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:20,080
Yes, at this point, the line representing shooting center rises in relative position.

378
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:21,200
Not only that,

379
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:22,639
because your ball speed is extremely fast,

380
00:16:22,639 --> 00:16:25,240
even if the goalkeeper stands still without moving,

381
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:26,600
he might not be able to react in time.

382
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:27,639
I see,

383
00:16:27,639 --> 00:16:30,639
so in the finely-tuned new model,

384
00:16:30,639 --> 00:16:35,560
shooting center suddenly becomes a completely reasonable best response.

385
00:16:35,560 --> 00:16:38,080
Game theory is by no means a rigid dogma;

386
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:41,759
it keeps evolving as real-world variables change.

387
00:16:41,759 --> 00:16:44,279
This makes the discipline come alive.

388
00:16:44,279 --> 00:16:46,120
Okay, now we know,

389
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:47,960
for a single action by the opponent,

390
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:50,679
how to find our best response.

391
00:16:50,679 --> 00:16:53,519
Now, push this logic to its extreme.

392
00:16:53,519 --> 00:16:55,200
If in the entire game,

393
00:16:55,200 --> 00:16:59,240
every player is simultaneously making best responses to others' choices,

394
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:00,200
what would happen?

395
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:01,399
Good question.

396
00:17:01,399 --> 00:17:05,319
This finally brings us to the ultimate boss of today's deep dive:

397
00:17:05,319 --> 00:17:06,400
the Nash equilibrium.

398
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:07,839
Nash equilibrium,

399
00:17:07,839 --> 00:17:10,440
this term sounds really lofty and mysterious.

400
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:11,599
Sounds intimidating.

401
00:17:11,599 --> 00:17:17,240
But we can use a very down-to-earth partner game to peel it open.

402
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:20,359
Imagine two law partners who founded a law firm together,

403
00:17:20,359 --> 00:17:22,039
or, as everyone is very familiar with,

404
00:17:22,039 --> 00:17:25,119
two students staying up late together doing a group assignment.

405
00:17:25,119 --> 00:17:27,319
Again the nightmare of homework.

406
00:17:27,319 --> 00:17:28,199
The rule is,

407
00:17:28,199 --> 00:17:30,159
the final profit or score of the project

408
00:17:30,159 --> 00:17:32,559
is split 50-50 between the two.

409
00:17:32,559 --> 00:17:35,559
But all the effort put into this project,

410
00:17:35,559 --> 00:17:38,919
whether it's researching materials, writing code, or losing hair staying up late,

411
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:43,160
this painful cost is borne 100% by each person individually.

412
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:46,600
Of course, the two working together also have the so-called synergy effect,

413
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:48,480
that is, one plus one is greater than two.

414
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:49,120
Right,

415
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:51,320
but the most realistic part of this setting is that

416
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:53,000
everyone is secretly calculating,

417
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,840
since the final outcome is split half and half,

418
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:56,800
why not do a little less myself,

419
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,320
and let the other person do a bit more?

420
00:17:58,320 --> 00:17:59,680
Yeah, this is too real.

421
00:17:59,680 --> 00:18:02,920
In economics, this is called externality.

422
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:05,360
When you're sitting in front of the computer at two in the morning,

423
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:08,120
considering whether to spend one more hour

424
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:10,400
polishing the report to make it more perfect,

425
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:12,840
the mental arithmetic in your head goes like this.

426
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:15,560
The exhaustion of staying up this extra hour

427
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:18,320
is borne 100% by myself.

428
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:21,480
But the extra value created by this hour,

429
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:24,400
half of it is given away for free to my partner.

430
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:28,000
Because you can't exclusively enjoy all the returns of your extra effort.

431
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,160
Rational calculation will prompt you to stop your current work,

432
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:32,840
and go slack off or sleep.

433
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:36,160
So, in this mutual-slacking probing,

434
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:39,000
where exactly is the Nash equilibrium hidden?

435
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,640
Imagine you two are now staring at the same shared document.

436
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:45,960
You see your partner hasn't typed for a long time,

437
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:47,560
you think he's slacking off,

438
00:18:47,560 --> 00:18:49,160
so you also slow down your pace.

439
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,120
This is you making a best response.

440
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:53,840
Haha, then when my partner sees me slowing down,

441
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:55,640
he definitely doesn't want to lose out.

442
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:58,720
Right, so he further reduces his level of effort,

443
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:00,640
also his best response.

444
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:03,200
You both keep adjusting your bottom lines based on

445
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:05,279
each other's level of laziness.

446
00:19:05,279 --> 00:19:07,960
Until you both fall to a critical point.

447
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,799
What kind of critical point?

448
00:19:09,799 --> 00:19:13,319
At this point, the project can barely pass.

449
00:19:13,319 --> 00:19:15,079
If you do a little less,

450
00:19:15,079 --> 00:19:17,759
the project completely fails and everyone is doomed.

451
00:19:17,759 --> 00:19:19,039
But if you do a little more,

452
00:19:19,039 --> 00:19:20,680
you feel it's too much of a loss.

453
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:25,399
In other words, my partner is having exactly the same thought right now.

454
00:19:25,399 --> 00:19:28,240
In this early morning of staying up to the brink of collapse,

455
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:30,519
neither of us is willing to type one more word,

456
00:19:30,519 --> 00:19:33,399
and neither dares to just shut down the computer and go to sleep.

457
00:19:33,399 --> 00:19:34,639
It's this moment.

458
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:38,040
This intersection where you both make best responses to each other,

459
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,640
is the Nash equilibrium.

460
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:43,280
At this point, something extremely decisive happens.

461
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:44,240
What?

462
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:48,080
Neither side has any incentive to unilaterally change its strategy.

463
00:19:48,080 --> 00:19:50,280
Whoever moves first loses.

464
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,000
This is the most core definition of the Nash equilibrium.

465
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,759
It's an extremely stable,

466
00:19:54,759 --> 00:19:57,600
structural stalemate that no one is willing to actively break.

467
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:01,080
Wow, this completely overturns my previous understanding.

468
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:03,680
I used to think that since it's called an equilibrium,

469
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:05,519
it must be some kind of perfect harmony,

470
00:20:05,519 --> 00:20:07,640
an ideal state where everyone is happy.

471
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:09,640
Many beginners think this way.

472
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:14,000
But it turns out, the Nash equilibrium doesn't mean the best outcome at all.

473
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,680
Like the prisoner's dilemma we mentioned at the very beginning,

474
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,240
where everyone falls into double loss,

475
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,920
that is also a Nash equilibrium.

476
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:21,480
A very thorough insight.

477
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:24,560
It merely means that under the current game rules,

478
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:29,600
everyone is stuck dead in a quagmire where no one dares to stick their head out.

479
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:30,680
This is not heaven.

480
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:33,400
This is basically a trap that locks everyone in.

481
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:36,560
Great summary. Looking back at our journey,

482
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:39,080
from the ineffective talk in the prisoner's dilemma,

483
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:42,720
to eliminating dominated strategies in Hannibal's story and the numbers game,

484
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:46,840
to the best responses in gas station locations and penalty shootouts.

485
00:20:46,840 --> 00:20:51,160
Finally, we unveiled the veil of the stalemate inside the Nash equilibrium.

486
00:20:51,160 --> 00:20:52,280
Right.

487
00:20:52,280 --> 00:20:56,880
You'll find that game theory is by no means just a theory in an ivory tower.

488
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:59,200
It's a pair of X-ray glasses for seeing through human interaction,

489
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:03,040
business competition, and everyday decision-making.

490
00:21:03,079 --> 00:21:05,079
Master it, and the world you see

491
00:21:05,079 --> 00:21:07,920
is no longer a series of isolated coincidences.

492
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:11,879
but countless interlocking gears and calculations.

493
00:21:11,879 --> 00:21:13,039
Indeed.

494
00:21:13,039 --> 00:21:16,119
I hope the hardcore logic we've sorted out for you today,

495
00:21:16,119 --> 00:21:19,599
can become, the next time you face a critical decision,

496
00:21:19,599 --> 00:21:22,319
even if it's just in the next group assignment,

497
00:21:22,319 --> 00:21:24,440
when you're strategizing against your partner,

498
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:26,839
your most powerful weapon of thought.

499
00:21:26,839 --> 00:21:29,319
Remember, don't just look at what others say,

500
00:21:29,319 --> 00:21:32,200
look at the payoffs and incentives they face.

501
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:34,559
Right, payoffs are the key.

502
00:21:34,559 --> 00:21:36,000
Before we end today,

503
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,039
I want to throw out a thought experiment,

504
00:21:38,039 --> 00:21:40,600
also from the Yale after-class exercises,

505
00:21:40,600 --> 00:21:42,000
for you to solve on your own.

506
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:43,440
Oh, this sounds quite interesting.

507
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,799
Yes, in the world of game theory, holding an advantage,

508
00:21:46,799 --> 00:21:48,480
or rather, power in hand,

509
00:21:48,480 --> 00:21:50,759
is it really always a good thing?

510
00:21:50,759 --> 00:21:53,720
Imagine a committee of three people.

511
00:21:53,720 --> 00:21:57,160
They're voting to select this year's best art award.

512
00:21:57,160 --> 00:22:00,559
There are three candidate works: A, B, and C.

513
00:22:00,559 --> 00:22:03,399
Three people voting for three works can easily end in a tie.

514
00:22:03,399 --> 00:22:07,599
Right, to prevent a 1-1-1 tie deadlock in the voting,

515
00:22:07,599 --> 00:22:09,720
the rules specially grant committee member A

516
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:11,599
an enormous privilege.

517
00:22:11,599 --> 00:22:14,720
Once there's a tie, the final result will be completely decided

518
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:17,279
by that single vote of member A.

519
00:22:17,279 --> 00:22:20,039
It sounds like member A controls the whole stage.

520
00:22:20,039 --> 00:22:20,879
On the surface,

521
00:22:20,879 --> 00:22:23,720
he does have absolute overwhelming power,

522
00:22:23,720 --> 00:22:27,319
but don't forget, the other two members are no pushovers.

523
00:22:27,319 --> 00:22:29,879
Precisely because they clearly know that member A

524
00:22:29,880 --> 00:22:33,800
holds this privilege, and know which work A favors most,

525
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:36,760
so to prevent A's favorite work from ultimately winning,

526
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:38,800
they will strategically ally in advance.

527
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:41,720
Right, through clever tactical voting,

528
00:22:41,720 --> 00:22:45,640
they directly kill A's favorite option in the very first round.

529
00:22:45,640 --> 00:22:48,840
This problem has a very thought-provoking name in the course materials:

530
00:22:48,840 --> 00:22:51,640
'power is exactly your weakness'.

531
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:53,640
The absolute advantage granted to you by the rules,

532
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:57,200
because it exposes your threat in advance, instead forces others to ally.

533
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:59,600
making you the biggest target.

534
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:00,600
What about you?

535
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:03,600
In real-world workplace or life games,

536
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:05,440
what position are you in?

537
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:08,080
Those privileges and advantages you take pride in,

538
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:11,160
could they be exactly the origin point where others scheme against you?

539
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:12,600
Before your next decision,

540
00:23:12,600 --> 00:23:15,880
why not try to thoroughly stand in someone else's shoes and look,

541
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:17,880
see you next time in our deep dive.